The '''2019 NBA draft''' was held on June 20, 2019. It took place at [[Barclays Center]] in [[Brooklyn|Brooklyn, New York]]. [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA) teams took turns selecting amateur United States [[college basketball]] players and other eligible players, including international players. It was televised nationally on [[NBA on ESPN|ESPN]]. [[State Farm]] was the presenting sponsor of the NBA draft for the eighth consecutive year.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sportsbusinessdaily.com/Daily/Issues/2019/06/20/Marketing-and-Sponsorship/Draft-Activation.aspx|title=Many League Sponsors Getting Involved With NBA Draft|website=sportsbusinessdaily.com|date=June 30, 2019|access-date=June 30, 2019|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128130725/https://www.sportsbusinessdaily.com/Daily/Issues/2019/06/20/Marketing-and-Sponsorship/Draft-Activation.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref> This draft was the first to feature a new weighted [[NBA draft lottery|lottery]] system in which the three worst teams each had a 14 percent chance of winning the lottery;<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/article/2017/09/28/nba-board-governors-approves-changes-draft-lottery-system|title=NBA Board of Governors approves changes to draft lottery system|website=NBA.com|date=September 28, 2017|access-date=November 16, 2018|archive-date=October 1, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201001141858/https://www.nba.com/article/2017/09/28/nba-board-governors-approves-changes-draft-lottery-system|url-status=live}}</ref> these teams were the [[New York Knicks]], [[Cleveland Cavaliers]], and [[Phoenix Suns]]. The lottery took place on May 14, during the [[NBA playoffs]]. Three of the four teams who held the top four picks of the draft this year rose up from at least six spots in the lottery, including the [[New Orleans Pelicans]], who won the first pick with 6 percent odds.<ref>{{cite web |last=Goldich |first=Mitch |title=Don't Be a Fool: Tanking Is Not Dead |url=https://www.si.com/nba/2019/05/15/nba-draft-lottery-lebron-james-anthony-davis-lakers-pelicans-tanking-not-dead |website=SI.com |access-date=May 20, 2019 |date=May 15, 2019 |archive-date=July 16, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210716201033/https://www.si.com/nba/2019/05/15/nba-draft-lottery-lebron-james-anthony-davis-lakers-pelicans-tanking-not-dead |url-status=live }}</ref> The Pelicans used that pick on [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke]] forward [[Zion Williamson]].
After Williamson, his college teammates R.J. Barrett and Cam Reddish were drafted in the top 10 as part of a talented Duke roster.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-06-21 |title=Williamson Leads Trio of Blue Devils Drafted in Top 10 |url=https://goduke.com/news/2019/6/21/211805710.aspx |access-date=2024-08-16 |website=Duke University |language=en}}</ref>  This draft included the first Japanese player to be selected in the first round, as well as the first Angolan player to be selected.

==Draft selections==
[[File:Zion Williamson 2020.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Zion Williamson]] was selected first overall by the [[New Orleans Pelicans]]. He is one of three [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke]] players drafted as lottery picks.]]
[[File:Ja Morant dribbling.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Ja Morant]] was selected second overall by the [[Memphis Grizzlies]]. He went on to win [[NBA Rookie of the Year]] honors for the 2019–20 season.]]
[[File:RJ Barrett Knicks.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[RJ Barrett]], another [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke]] player, was selected third overall by the [[New York Knicks]].]]
[[File:Darius Garland 2021-2022.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Darius Garland]] was selected fifth overall by the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]].]]
[[File:White with the Chicago Bulls on January 2022.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Coby White]] was selected seventh overall by the [[Chicago Bulls]].]]
[[File:Tyler Herro.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Tyler Herro]] was selected thirteenth overall by the [[Miami Heat]].]]
[[File:Goga Bitadze (cropped).jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Goga Bitadze]] was selected eighteenth overall by the [[Indiana Pacers]].]]
[[File: Jordan Poole (Michigan Wolverines men's basketball player) during December 4, 2018 game against Northwestern Wildcats at Welsh-Ryan Arena in Evanston, Illinois (14).jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Jordan Poole]] was selected twenty-eighth overall by the [[Golden State Warriors]].]]
[[File:Nicolas Claxton (49165813553) (cropped).jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Nicolas Claxton]] was selected thirty-first overall by the [[Brooklyn Nets]].]]
[[File:Tmann.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Terance Mann]] was selected forty-eighth overall by the [[Los Angeles Clippers]].]]
